=== Does the role-A proof amortise across swaps? ===

Master seed   : 6c61732d737761702f657072696e742d323032302d3834352f666967312f7631
Seed source   : pinned default
Proof system  : Groth16 over BN254
Relation      : exists r : A r = t and ||r||inf <= 1   (per instance)
Public input  : 1536 field elements per instance
Repetitions   : 5 per batch size (statistical floor 5)

  measured k = 1
  measured k = 2
  measured k = 4
  measured k = 8

--- Cost of ONE proof covering k statements (totals) ---

    k          setup (ms)          prove (ms)       verify (us)   proof (B)
    1       673.5 +- 22.0       591.1 +- 29.8   12571.2 +- 311.8         128
    2      1447.6 +- 111.9      1166.3 +- 140.7   24374.4 +- 505.5         128
    4      3083.0 +- 261.4      2668.1 +- 173.2   47583.9 +- 1251.7         128
    8      6379.2 +- 547.3      5140.0 +- 210.7   96260.8 +- 12375.9         128

--- Amortised PER SWAP (total / k) ---

    k      prove (ms)     verify (us)       proof (B)      proof vs k=1
    1           591.1         12571.2           128.0             1.00x
    2           583.1         12187.2            64.0             0.50x
    4           667.0         11896.0            32.0             0.25x
    8           642.5         12032.6            16.0             0.12x

--- What this shows ---

Proof size is the only column that amortises, and it amortises perfectly:
one Groth16 proof is three group elements regardless of how many instances
it covers, so k = 8 carries the same 128 B as k = 1 and the per-swap share
falls to 16.0 B.

Proving does not amortise: per-swap proving went from 591.1 ms at k = 1 to
642.5 ms at k = 8. The constraint system grows with the batch, so the work
is paid per instance either way -- batching moves it, it does not remove it.

Verification does not amortise either: the public input is 1536 field
elements per instance, so a verifier of a k-batch reads k times as much.

The practical reading: batching helps exactly where the swap is
communication-bound and not where it is compute-bound. The swap study found
the proof dominant in BOTH, so batching addresses one half of the problem
and leaves the other untouched. It also changes the protocol: a batch must
be proved before any of its statements is used, so a party has to know its
next k swaps in advance, and every counterparty verifies the whole batch to
use one statement of it.

SCOPE: Groth16 only, and Groth16 is not post-quantum -- it is configuration
2's prover, kept as the controlled comparison against LaZer. Nothing here
measures LaZer, whose proof grows with the relation, and nothing here is a
security claim about batching.
